{#
  For a reader who does not touch the cloud console.

  Deliberately does not list findings. An executive summary that ends in a
  four-hundred-row table is a technical report with a cover page, and the
  question it is meant to answer -- how exposed are we, and is it getting
  better -- gets buried in the answer to a different one.
#}
{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% set title = "Executive security report" %}

{% block body %}

{% include "_posture.html.j2" %}

{% if 'top_risks' in report.sections %}
<h2>What is worst right now</h2>
{% if report.top_risks %}
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr><th>Risk</th><th class="num">Score</th></tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for risk in report.top_risks %}
    <tr>
      <td>
        <span class="sev sev-{{ risk.risk_level }}">{{ risk.risk_level | title }}</span>
        {{ risk.title }}
      </td>
      <td class="num">{{ risk.risk_score | round(0) | int }}</td>
    </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>
<p class="small muted">
  A finding is what CloudGuard observed. A risk is what that observation means
  for this asset, with this data, at this level of exposure — which is why the
  order here is not the order of severity alone.
</p>
{% else %}
<p class="muted">No risks are currently ranked for this organization.</p>
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

{% if 'attack_paths' in report.sections %}
{% include "_attack_paths.html.j2" %}
{% endif %}

{% if 'remediation' in report.sections %}
{% include "_remediation.html.j2" %}
{% endif %}

{% if 'compliance' in report.sections %}
{% include "_compliance.html.j2" %}
{% endif %}

{% endblock %}
